Title: "Logic"
Id: "2675f869-5535-45d3-9009-0a76ffa07961"


Text: "Before any mathematics can be stated precisely, we need a small
       vocabulary for combining and relating statements. This opening chapter
       records that vocabulary: the logical connectives that turn one or two
       statements into a new one. Everything here operates on statements alone,
       independently of what those statements happen to be about, so the
       connectives defined below are used unchanged in every later chapter."
Id: "b03f7ce2-7aed-4c81-a6e7-d998ccf65747"


SectionTitle: "Conditionals and Equivalence"
Id: "706c5ccb-8690-478e-b0fd-90e906f0276e"


Text: "We begin with the conditional, the connective that expresses logical
       consequence. It is the backbone of every definition and proof that
       follows, since to prove a theorem is almost always to show that some
       conclusion follows from a collection of hypotheses."
Id: "7529a4ce-e5cc-46c7-85b9-fdcd19351351"


[P \.implies./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. if: P
  then: Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ implies $Q?$"
. written: "P? \Rightarrow Q?"
. description: "The conditional *if $P$ then $Q$*, written $P \Rightarrow Q$. It
                asserts that whenever $P$ holds, $Q$ holds as well. It makes no
                claim at all in the case that $P$ fails, so a conditional with a
                false hypothesis is regarded as holding vacuously."
Id: "dc7e213d-76c2-43d7-acf1-c5978daaab33"


Text: "When each of two statements implies the other, they stand or fall
       together. This mutual implication is the biconditional, and it is the
       form in which most definitions and characterizations are cast."
Id: "b60eca2f-6be8-4e23-a2f2-b56e2378684b"


[P \.iff./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. have: P
  iff: Q
Documented:
. called:
  . "$P?$ if and only if $Q?$"
  . "$P?$ iff $Q?$"
. written: "P? \iff Q?"
. description: "The biconditional of $P$ and $Q$, read *$P$ if and only if $Q$*.
                It holds exactly when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value --
                each implies the other -- and it is the connective used to state
                that two conditions are equivalent."
Id: "96440ac7-3331-4467-a7ac-593dbca0761b"


SectionTitle: "Boolean Connectives"
Id: "faacb189-b227-404c-8a8a-b6e976fc828a"


Text: "The primary Boolean connectives build compound statements from simpler
       ones. Conjunction asserts that two statements both hold, disjunction that
       at least one of them does, and negation that a statement fails."
Id: "1d61c6bd-bf4c-4c3e-a6dd-769d5c662a6f"


[P \.and./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. allOf:
  . P
  . Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ and $Q?$"
. written: "P? \land Q?"
. description: "The conjunction of $P$ and $Q$. The statement $P \text{ and } Q$
                holds precisely when $P$ holds and $Q$ holds; if either fails,
                the conjunction fails."
Id: "e024d041-fcf1-476e-b29e-b75d88b04fa8"


[P \.or./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that:
. anyOf:
  . P
  . Q
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ or $Q?$"
. written: "P? \lor Q?"
. description: "The disjunction of $P$ and $Q$. The statement $P \text{ or } Q$
                holds when at least one of $P$ and $Q$ holds. As is standard in
                mathematics, the *or* is inclusive: it also holds when both do."
Id: "fd7a79c3-4380-4621-a699-eb6902667669"


[\not{P}]
States:
when: P is \\statement
that:
. not: P
Documented:
. called: "not $P?$"
. written: "\neg P+?"
. description: "The negation of $P$, written $\neg P$. It holds exactly when $P$
                does not, and so reverses the truth value of the statement it is
                applied to."
Id: "856ad9cc-c17e-4cff-ad32-10ee78ab20c4"


Text: "The connectives above suffice to define any others by combination. As one
       example we record the exclusive *or*, which holds when the two statements
       differ in truth value -- that is, when one of them holds but not both."
Id: "009e13c8-8dba-4b31-9958-f8531eb5ec04"


[P \.xor./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: (P \.or./ Q) \.and./ \not{P \.and./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ xor $Q?$"
. written: "P? \text{ xor } Q?"
. description: "The exclusive disjunction of $P$ and $Q$. It holds when $P$ or
                $Q$ holds but *not* both, and is defined here as $(P \text{ or }
                Q) \text{ and } \neg(P \text{ and } Q)$ -- inclusive *or* with
                the both-true case removed."
Id: "1c7bc638-0db8-416f-a0d0-f5c0d7d92842"


Text: "Two other notable derived connectives are the Sheffer stroke (NAND) and
       the Peirce arrow (NOR). Each of these connectives is functionally
       complete on its own, meaning that every truth-functional operator can be
       expressed purely in terms of NAND alone or NOR alone."
Id: "2d5efb63-58bb-436f-9aa6-648a2c05e6b6"


[P \.nand./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: \not{P \.and./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ nand $Q?$"
. written: "P? \barwedge Q?"
. description: "The alternative denial (Sheffer stroke) of $P$ and $Q$. It is
                true if and only if at least one of the propositions is false,
                failing only when both $P$ and $Q$ are simultaneously true."
Id: "ac552ed6-48a1-4639-940a-9d9a291c76e4"


[P \.nor./ Q]
States:
when: P, Q is \\statement
that: \not{P \.or./ Q}
Documented:
. called: "$P?$ nor $Q?$"
. written: "P? \downarrow Q?"
. description: "The joint denial (Peirce arrow) of $P$ and $Q$. It asserts that
                neither $P$ nor $Q$ holds, being true precisely when both
                statements are false."
Id: "a16193ad-6084-494c-afd2-b4f7c6a70a7d"


SectionTitle: "Fundamental Laws of Propositional Logic"
Id: "1b3ef4fc-1add-407c-84c2-ac7234d8e0f9"


Text: "Classical propositional logic satisfies several fundamental algebraic
       laws. The first is the law of double negation, which states that denying
       a negated proposition returns the original proposition."
Id: "ddf66eda-3e4a-4b16-a628-eaa5d5dbc270"


Theorem:
given: P is \\statement
then: \not{\not{P}} \.iff./ P
Documented:
. called: "Law of Double Negation"
. description: "A proposition $P$ is logically equivalent to the negation of its
                negation $\neg(\neg P)$."
Id: "eb21571a-d245-427d-a014-d29407d09f9a"


Text: "De Morgan's laws describe the duality between conjunction and disjunction
       under negation: the negation of a conjunction is the disjunction of the
       negations, and the negation of a disjunction is the conjunction of the
       negations."
Id: "2f552add-aa29-4824-b7d0-40345e29c312"


Theorem:
given: P, Q is \\statement
then:
. \not{P \.and./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.or./ \not{Q})
. \not{P \.or./ Q} \.iff./ (\not{P} \.and./ \not{Q})
Documented:
. called: "De Morgan's Laws for Logic"
. description: "Negation interchanges conjunction and disjunction: $\neg(P \land Q)
                \iff (\neg P \lor \neg Q)$ and $\neg(P \lor Q) \iff (\neg P \land \neg Q)$."
Id: "e4c6ef38-dd37-448b-a1f5-377df32138ce"
